    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>FHA loan requirements in Texas are the same national rules HUD sets everywhere: a 580 credit score for 3.5% down (500 to 579 with 10% down), a manageable debt-to-income ratio, upfront and annual mortgage insurance, a home you will live in, and a loan amount within your county's limit. For 2026, that limit is $541,287 for a single-family home in Harris County and the surrounding Houston counties.</p>
<p>Below is each requirement in plain English, with the Texas and Houston details that affect your purchase.</p>

<h2>FHA credit score and down payment requirements</h2>
<p>HUD ties your minimum down payment, which it calls the minimum required investment, to your credit score:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Credit score</th><th>Minimum down payment</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>580 or higher</td><td>3.5%</td></tr>
<tr><td>500 to 579</td><td>10%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Below 500</td><td>Not eligible</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Those are HUD's floors. Individual lenders often add stricter "overlays," so one lender may decline a 590 score that another approves. If your score is near a cutoff, talk to more than one lender.</p>
<p>Your down payment can come from savings, a documented gift, or an approved assistance program. FHA accepts gifts from family members, your employer or union, a close friend with a documented interest in you, a charity, or a government agency or public entity with a homeownership program. Cash on hand that you cannot trace is not acceptable, and a donor must sign a gift letter. Our guide to <a href="/blog/down-payment-assistance-texas/">down payment assistance in Texas</a> covers the state and Houston-area programs that pair with FHA loans.</p>

<h2>Debt-to-income ratio</h2>
<p>FHA looks at two ratios: your housing payment divided by gross monthly income (front-end) and all monthly debts including the new mortgage divided by gross income (back-end).</p>
<p>Most FHA loans are approved through FHA's automated underwriting scorecard, which can accept higher ratios when the rest of your file is strong. When a loan is manually underwritten, HUD's handbook sets these limits:</p>
<ul>
<li>Scores of 500 to 579 or no credit score: 31% front-end and 43% back-end.</li>
<li>Scores of 580 and above: 31/43 with no compensating factors, up to 37/47 with one compensating factor, and up to 40/50 with two (such as verified cash reserves, a minimal increase in housing payment, or residual income).</li>
</ul>
<p>In Texas, property taxes and homeowners insurance make up a big share of the monthly payment, so they push your front-end ratio up faster than in many states. Check where you stand with our <a href="/tools/dti-calculator/">DTI calculator</a> before you apply.</p>

<h2>FHA mortgage insurance (MIP)</h2>
<p>Every FHA loan carries two mortgage insurance charges, regardless of credit score.</p>
<h3>Upfront MIP</h3>
<p>The upfront premium is 1.75% of the base loan amount. Most borrowers finance it into the loan rather than paying it in cash at closing.</p>
<h3>Annual MIP</h3>
<p>The annual premium is divided into monthly payments. For loans with terms longer than 15 years and a base loan of $726,200 or less, the rates set by HUD in 2023 are:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Down payment (LTV)</th><th>Annual MIP</th><th>How long it lasts</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Less than 5% down (LTV above 95%)</td><td>0.55%</td><td>Life of the loan</td></tr>
<tr><td>5% to under 10% down (LTV above 90% to 95%)</td><td>0.50%</td><td>Life of the loan</td></tr>
<tr><td>10% or more down (LTV 90% or less)</td><td>0.50%</td><td>11 years</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Rates are lower on 15-year loans and higher on loans above $726,200. Here is a rough example: on a $300,000 Houston home with 3.5% down, the base loan is $289,500, the upfront premium is about $5,066, and the annual premium works out to about $130 a month in the first year. Plug your own numbers into our <a href="/tools/mortgage-calculator/">mortgage calculator</a>.</p>
<p>Because MIP usually lasts the life of the loan with less than 10% down, many FHA borrowers later refinance into a conventional loan once they have enough equity.</p>

<h2>2026 FHA loan limits in Houston and Texas</h2>
<p>FHA limits for 2026 apply to case numbers assigned on or after January 1, 2026. The national floor for a one-unit home is $541,287 and the ceiling for the highest-cost areas is $1,249,125. HUD's loan limit lookup shows the Houston-area counties at the floor:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>County (2026)</th><th>1 unit</th><th>2 units</th><th>3 units</th><th>4 units</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Harris, Fort Bend, Montgomery, Galveston, Brazoria</td><td>$541,287</td><td>$693,050</td><td>$837,700</td><td>$1,041,125</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Most of Texas uses the floor limits, but a few higher-cost counties sit above it. For example, HUD lists Travis County at $571,550 and Collin County at $563,500 for a one-unit home in 2026. If you are buying outside the Houston area, look up your county on HUD's site.</p>
<p>The limit caps the loan amount, not the purchase price. You can buy a more expensive home with an FHA loan if your down payment covers the difference.</p>

<h2>Property and appraisal requirements</h2>
<p>An FHA loan requires an appraisal by an appraiser on FHA's roster. Beyond estimating value, the appraiser checks that the home meets HUD's Minimum Property Requirements, which boil down to the home being safe, sound and secure.</p>
<p>Items an FHA appraiser looks at include:</p>
<ul>
<li>A roof that keeps moisture out and has reasonable remaining life</li>
<li>Visible foundation or structural issues, which can trigger a further inspection</li>
<li>Defective exterior paint on newer homes, and lead-based paint rules for homes built before 1979</li>
<li>Working mechanical systems, including a water heater with a temperature and pressure relief valve</li>
<li>A look inside the attic</li>
</ul>
<p>When the appraiser notes required repairs, they typically must be completed or otherwise addressed before closing. That matters on older Houston homes and as-is sales, where a seller may not want to fix anything. An FHA appraisal is not a home inspection, so still hire a TREC-licensed inspector during your option period; our <a href="/blog/home-inspection-checklist/">home inspection checklist</a> shows what to cover.</p>

<h2>Occupancy, residency and other borrower rules</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Owner occupancy.</strong> At least one borrower must move in within 60 days of closing and intend to live there for at least one year. FHA is for primary residences, not vacation homes or pure rentals.</li>
<li><strong>One- to four-unit homes.</strong> You can buy a duplex, triplex or fourplex if you live in one unit.</li>
<li><strong>Residency.</strong> Since May 25, 2025, FHA eligibility is limited to U.S. citizens and lawful permanent residents. Non-permanent residents are no longer eligible.</li>
<li><strong>Seller concessions.</strong> Sellers and other interested parties can contribute up to 6% of the sales price toward closing costs, prepaids and similar items, but not toward your minimum down payment.</li>
<li><strong>Steady income.</strong> Lenders verify employment and income history, generally looking back about two years.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Waiting periods after bankruptcy, foreclosure and short sale</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Standard FHA waiting period</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Chapter 7 bankruptcy</td><td>2 years from discharge (12 to 24 months possible with documented extenuating circumstances)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chapter 13 bankruptcy</td><td>12 months of on-time plan payments, plus written permission from the bankruptcy court</td></tr>
<tr><td>Foreclosure or deed in lieu</td><td>3 years from the transfer of the property</td></tr>
<tr><td>Short sale</td><td>3 years, with an exception if you were current on the mortgage for the 12 months before the sale</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>HUD allows exceptions for documented circumstances beyond your control, such as serious illness or the death of a wage earner, but divorce alone does not qualify. You also need to show you have re-established good credit since the event.</p>

<h2>FHA vs conventional loans</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>FHA</th><th>Conventional</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Minimum down payment</td><td>3.5% (580+ score)</td><td>As low as 3% on some programs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Credit flexibility</td><td>Scores down to 500 with 10% down</td><td>Lenders commonly want higher scores, and pricing rises as scores fall</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mortgage insurance</td><td>1.75% upfront plus annual MIP, often for the life of the loan</td><td>Private mortgage insurance with less than 20% down; can be canceled as you build equity</td></tr>
<tr><td>2026 loan limit (Harris County, 1 unit)</td><td>$541,287</td><td>$832,750 conforming limit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Property standards</td><td>Must meet HUD Minimum Property Requirements</td><td>Appraisal focuses mainly on value and condition</td></tr>
<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Lower scores, smaller down payments, higher debt ratios</td><td>Stronger credit, larger down payments, higher-priced homes</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Ask lenders to quote both. With a strong score and at least 5% down, a conventional loan is often cheaper over time because private mortgage insurance can come off. With a lower score, FHA usually wins.</p>
<p>These are general guidelines, not financial advice; confirm your own eligibility with a licensed mortgage lender.</p>

<h2>Documents to gather before you apply</h2>
<p>FHA lenders verify everything, so having your paperwork ready speeds up pre-approval and underwriting:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Income.</strong> Recent pay stubs and W-2s from the previous two years. Self-employed borrowers should expect to provide two years of tax returns.</li>
<li><strong>Employment history.</strong> Names and dates for your employers over the last two years, with explanations for any gaps.</li>
<li><strong>Assets.</strong> Recent bank statements for every account you will use for the down payment and closing costs. Large deposits that are not payroll will need a paper trail.</li>
<li><strong>Gift letter.</strong> If family is helping, a signed letter stating the amount and that no repayment is expected, plus proof of the transfer.</li>
<li><strong>Credit explanations.</strong> Bankruptcy discharge papers, foreclosure dates or letters explaining late payments, if they apply.</li>
<li><strong>Identification and residency.</strong> A valid ID and, for permanent residents, proof of lawful permanent residency.</li>
</ul>
<p>Avoid opening new credit or making big purchases between pre-approval and closing. A new car payment can change your debt-to-income ratio and stall underwriting.</p>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>The core FHA loan requirements in Texas are a 580 score for 3.5% down, a debt load your lender can approve, mortgage insurance you budget for, a home that meets HUD's property standards, and a loan amount no higher than $541,287 for a single-family home in the Houston area in 2026. Get quotes from more than one FHA lender, compare against a conventional loan, and look into down payment assistance before you make an offer.</p>
